<p>The core observation is simple but powerful: model-specific harness tuning yields substantial gains, but human expert tuning doesn't scale. Self-Harness asks: <em>can the model tune its own harness?</em> The answer is yes — and it does so by grounding every proposed edit in behavioral evidence from execution traces, not hunches.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — Mining Failure Patterns -->
<section id="s-mining">
<div class="section-no">02 — Mining Failure Patterns</div>
<h2>Failures Aren't Isolated — They're Clusters</h2>
<p>The first stage of Self-Harness is <strong>Weakness Mining</strong>. The key insight is that fixing an agent shouldn't be reactive to individual failures. Instead, recurring structural problems — things the model does wrong consistently — should be identified as clusters and addressed with targeted harness changes.</p>
<p>Each failed execution trace gets a <strong>failure signature</strong> φ(r) = (c, q, m): the verifier-level cause c (e.g., "artifact missing"), the agent behavior q that caused it (e.g., "continued exploring after deadline"), and the abstract mechanism m (e.g., "unbounded exploration loop"). Two traces are grouped only when all three components match — this prevents conflating superficial symptoms with distinct root causes.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Failure Signature</div>
  φ(r<sub>i</sub>) = (<span class="math-var">c</span>, <span class="math-var">q</span>, <span class="math-var">m</span>)
  <br><span class="math-comment">  c = terminal verifier cause (e.g., "artifact missing")</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">  q = causal agent behavior (e.g., "continued after timeout")</span>
  <br><span class="math-comment">  m = abstract mechanism (e.g., "unbounded exploration")</span>
  <br>
  C<sub>φ</sub> = &#123; r<sub>i</sub> ∈ F<sub>t</sub> | φ(r<sub>i</sub>) = φ &#125;
  <span class="math-comment">  ← same signature → same cluster → same harness fix</span>
</div>

<!-- Cluster canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">execution traces → failure clusters</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Play to watch traces cluster by root cause</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="cluster-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="cluster-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);align-self:center" id="cluster-label">scattered execution traces</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="cluster-canvas" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.02);border:1px solid var(--border)"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Clusters are ordered by support (how many traces share the pattern) and estimated actionability (whether a concrete harness surface can plausibly address it). The output is an <strong>evidence bundle</strong> — structured, evaluator-grounded failure patterns, not a dump of raw logs. This separation keeps the evaluator distinct from the optimizer while ensuring that proposed edits are motivated by cross-case evidence, not individual anecdotes.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why clustering matters:</strong> Two runs may fail with "timeout" but for different reasons — one because the model explored endlessly, another because a network call hung. Grouping them by φ(r) prevents the proposer from trying to write a single fix for structurally different problems.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 — The Self-Improvement Loop -->
<section id="s-loop">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Self-Improvement Loop</div>
<h2>Three Stages: Mine → Propose → Validate</h2>
<p>Self-Harness operationalizes self-improvement as a bounded, evidence-driven loop. The same fixed model participates in two roles: <em>agent</em> (running tasks and producing traces) and <em>proposer</em> (analyzing patterns and suggesting harness edits). Each iteration produces candidate harness variants that are tested before adoption — no edit is promoted just because the model finds it plausible.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="step-list">
  <div class="step-box" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Weakness Mining</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run the agent on the held-in task split. Collect execution traces with verifier outcomes. Cluster failed traces by failure signature φ(r) = (c, q, m). Build an evidence bundle of ranked, actionable failure patterns.</div>
      <div class="step-code">Bt ← BUILD_EVIDENCE_BUNDLE(Rt)  # from held-in failures</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Harness Proposal</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The same model — operating under the current harness in a "proposer role" — generates K parallel candidate edits. Each edit targets one failure mechanism, modifies only the needed harness surface, and includes an audit record explaining its expected behavioral effect.</div>
      <div class="step-code">Pt ← PARALLEL_PROPOSE(M, ht, Bt, K)  # K diverse, minimal edits</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Proposal Validation</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Each candidate harness is evaluated on both the held-in split (did it fix the target?) and the held-out split (did it regress anything?). A candidate is accepted only if it improves at least one split without degrading the other. Accepted edits are merged; rejected ones are logged.</div>
      <div class="step-code">if Δin ≥ 0 and Δho ≥ 0 and max(Δin, Δho) &gt; 0: ACCEPT</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 3</span>
</div>

<p>The acceptance rule is deliberately conservative: a candidate that improves held-in performance by 5% but degrades held-out by 1% is rejected. This prevents the proposer from overfitting its suggestions to the specific failures it observed. When multiple candidates pass in the same round, their edits are <strong>merged</strong> into the next harness, since they targeted different failure mechanisms and are likely complementary.</p>

<div class="callout callout-cyan">
  <strong>No stronger external agent required.</strong> Unlike Meta-Harness and ADAS, Self-Harness never calls a more powerful model to guide improvement. The same fixed model proposes edits based on its own behavioral evidence — making this feasible even for frontier models with no stronger peer available.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 — Evolution Trajectories -->
<section id="s-evolution">
<div class="section-no">04 — How Harnesses Evolve</div>
<h2>Same Starting Point, Different Paths</h2>
<p>All three models begin from the <strong>same minimal initial harness</strong> — a short benchmark system prompt plus default file/shell tools from the DeepAgent framework. Over 15–20 iterations, each model's harness evolves through a series of accepted and rejected candidate edits toward a model-specific endpoint.</p>
<p>The evolution is <em>not</em> a smooth ascent. Most proposals are rejected. A few accepted edits drive all the meaningful improvement. The staircase pattern below makes this concrete: flat stretches between accepted candidates, sudden steps upward at each successful validation.</p>

