</section>

<!-- §05 — Model-Specific Discoveries -->
<section id="s-specific">
<div class="section-no">05 — Model-Specific Discoveries</div>
<h2>Three Models, Three Distinct Fixes</h2>
<p>The most revealing result isn't the numbers — it's <em>what</em> Self-Harness discovered for each model. The three promoted harnesses have almost nothing in common beyond a shared concern for artifact creation. Each model's self-engineered harness reflects its unique failure mode.</p>

<table class="compare-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Model</th>
      <th>Dominant Failure Pattern</th>
      <th>Harness Fix</th>
      <th>Mechanism Changed</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">MiniMax M2.5</td>
      <td>Explores for 47+ steps without ever writing required output artifacts; times out</td>
      <td>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-p">create output early</span>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-p">schema content tags</span>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-p">loop breaker @ 50 calls</span>
      </td>
      <td>Bootstrap instruction + runtime tool limit</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">Qwen3.5‑35B‑A3B</td>
      <td>Creates required script, hits overwrite errors, retries indefinitely, then <em>deletes</em> the artifact before stopping</td>
      <td>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-c">dependency precheck</span>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-c">exploration loop breaker</span>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-c">artifact-ensure subagent</span>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-c">tool-error middleware</span>
      </td>
      <td>Middleware + subagent decomposition + retry discipline</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="mn">GLM-5</td>
      <td>Makes tool/environment changes that don't persist across shell sessions; spends too long on exploration before implementing</td>
      <td>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-g">persist env across sessions</span>
        <span class="fix-tag fix-g">exploration→implementation trigger</span>
      </td>
      <td>Execution instruction + verification-stage gate</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>These aren't generic "be more careful" instructions. MiniMax's runtime loop-breaker caps total tool messages and redirects execution. Qwen3.5's tool-error middleware intercepts failures and redirects the agent toward recovering the missing artifact rather than retrying the same failing command. GLM-5's change explicitly instructs the agent to use <code>export</code> or <code>source</code> to make environment modifications persistent — a terminal-specific behavioral constraint that wouldn't occur to a human engineer who didn't observe GLM-5's specific failure pattern.</p>

<div class="callout callout-green">
  <strong>Key finding:</strong> Self-Harness does not simply make the system prompt longer or add generic instructions. It introduces targeted changes that reflect the recurring problems each model encounters — turning model-specific weaknesses into concrete, executable harness mechanisms.
</div>

<p>Perhaps most striking is Qwen3.5's <strong>subagent-based decomposition</strong> — Self-Harness actually introduced a structural mechanism (a dedicated "artifact-ensure" subagent) that goes beyond local failure repair. This is the kind of architectural insight typically produced by senior engineers; here it emerged from behavioral evidence alone.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 — Results -->
<section id="s-results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Consistent Gains — Held-Out and Held-In</h2>
<p>Self-Harness was evaluated on <strong>Terminal-Bench-2.0</strong>, a 64-task benchmark of containerized terminal tasks with deterministic verifiers. The critical metric is the <strong>held-out</strong> pass rate: tasks whose execution traces were never shown to the proposer — proving that accepted edits generalize beyond the failure patterns they were designed to fix.</p>

