<p>The held-in gains are larger in most cases (agents also improve on the tasks whose failures motivated the edits), but the held-out gains are what matter most: <strong>no accepted harness degraded either split</strong>. The regression gate worked. The acceptance rule's conservatism — requiring non-regression on both splits — prevented harnesses that overfit to observed failure patterns from being promoted.</p>

<p>The 138% relative improvement for Qwen3.5 on the held-in split (15.1% → 36.0%) is the largest, reflecting how poorly the minimal initial harness suited this model's behavior. Qwen3.5 also starts from the lowest absolute baseline (23.8% held-out), suggesting its failure modes were most actionable through harness changes — particularly the retry discipline and artifact-recovery middleware.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>What Self-Harness doesn't claim:</strong> It studies bounded harness edits under fixed benchmarks, not open-ended self-improvement. Accepted edits may still reflect benchmark-specific patterns, and the protocol depends on verifier quality. Higher-stakes deployments would need stronger acceptance gates than pass-rate non-regression alone.
</div>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Self-Harness</h2>
    <p>Self-Harness is a new paradigm where a fixed LLM agent iteratively improves the harness it operates under — the system prompt, tools, and runtime policies — without relying on human engineers or stronger external models. It works through three stages: Weakness Mining clusters failed execution traces by failure signature to find recurring patterns; Harness Proposal generates targeted minimal edits using the same model in a proposer role; and Proposal Validation tests each edit against a regression gate, accepting only changes that improve performance on both held-in and held-out tasks.</p>
    <p>On Terminal-Bench-2.0, Self-Harness improved held-out pass rates for MiniMax M2.5 from 40.5% to 61.9%, Qwen3.5 from 23.8% to 38.1%, and GLM-5 from 42.9% to 57.1%. Each model received entirely different fixes: M2.5 needed a runtime loop-breaker and early artifact creation; Qwen3.5 needed middleware for tool-error recovery and retry discipline; GLM-5 needed explicit environment persistence across shell sessions. These model-specific differences confirm that Self-Harness discovers genuine behavioral failure modes rather than adding generic instructions.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：Self-Harness</h2>
    <p>Self-Harness 是一种全新范式，让固定的大语言模型代理通过迭代方式优化自身所运行的"harness"（包括系统提示、工具和运行策略），无需人类工程师或更强大的外部模型介入。其工作流程分为三个阶段：弱点挖掘阶段通过故障特征对失败执行轨迹进行聚类，找出反复出现的模式；Harness提案阶段让同一模型以"提案者"身份生成针对性的最小化修改方案；提案验证阶段对每个修改方案进行回归测试，仅接受在已见和未见任务上均有提升的方案。</p>
    <p>在Terminal-Bench-2.0基准测试中，Self-Harness将MiniMax M2.5的held-out通过率从40.5%提升至61.9%，Qwen3.5从23.8%提升至38.1%，GLM-5从42.9%提升至57.1%。三个模型获得了截然不同的修复方案：M2.5需要运行时循环限制器和提前创建制品的指令；Qwen3.5需要工具错误恢复中间件和重试管控；GLM-5需要跨shell会话的环境持久化指令。这些模型特异性差异证明，Self-Harness发现的是真实的行为缺陷，而非简单地添加通用指令。</p>
